  <page>
    <exercise xml:id="hw06-q1">
      <title>Question 1: Classification Tree on the OJ Data Set</title>
      <introduction>
      <p>
        First, perform the usual imports.
      </p>

        <program language="python">
          <code>
import numpy as np
import pandas as pd
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split, cross_val_score, StratifiedKFold
from sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeClassifier, plot_tree, export_text
from sklearn.metrics import confusion_matrix, ConfusionMatrixDisplay
from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestClassifier
</code>
        </program>

      <p>
        Load the OJ data set from <xref ref="OJ-data"/> into data frame <c>OJ_df</c>. Use <c>Purchase</c>  as the response and all other columns in the <c>OJ</c> data set as predictors.
      </p>
      <p>
        Convert the <c>Store7</c> column to a numeric column, where <sq>Yes</sq> is 1 and <sq>No</sq> is 0.
      </p>

      <program language="python">
        <code>
OJ_df["Store7"] = OJ_df["Store7"].map({"Yes": 1, "No": 0})
OJ_df.head()
          </code>
        </program>
      </introduction>

      <task xml:id="hw06-q1-a">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Create a training set containing a random sample of
            800 observations and a test set containing the remaining
            observations. Report the percentage in the training set.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="hw06-q1-b">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Fit a classification tree to the training data with
            maximum depth 3. Report the fitted model and its training
            error rate.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="hw06-q1-c-plot">
        <statement>
          <p> Plot the fitted tree.</p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="hw06-q1-c-interpret" workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            How many terminal nodes does the tree have?
            Which variables appear near its top? Briefly interpret
            two main splits.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="hw06-q1-d-export">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use <c>export_text()</c> to produce a text summary
            of the fitted tree.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="hw06-q1-d-interpret" workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Choose one terminal node. Describe the split path
            leading to it, the observations that fall there, and its
            predicted class.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="hw06-q1-e-matrix">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Predict <c>Purchase</c> on the test set. Produce a
            confusion matrix comparing the test labels to your
            predictions, and report the test error rate.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="hw06-q1-e-comment" workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Briefly comment on the model's performance using
            the confusion matrix and test error rate.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>
  </page>

  <page>
    <exercise xml:id="hw06-q2">
      <title>Question 2: Cross-Validation and Tree Size</title>
      <task xml:id="hw06-q2-a">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Using only the training set, evaluate classification
            trees with maximum depths 1 through 20 by five-fold
            cross-validation. Plot maximum depth against the mean
            cross-validated classification error rate.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task xml:id="hw06-q2-b" workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Which maximum depth has the lowest cross-validated
            error rate? Briefly explain your selection. Here, tree
            size is parameterized by maximum depth; it need not
            equal the number of terminal nodes.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>
  </page>

  <page>
    <exercise xml:id="hw06-q3">
      <title>Question 3: Unpruned and Restricted Trees</title>
      <task xml:id="hw06-q3-a">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Fit an unrestricted tree and a pruned or
            depth-restricted tree using the depth selected in Question 2.
            If cross-validation does not clearly support pruning, you may
            use a smaller reasonable tree and justify your choice.
            State the chosen depth or number of terminal nodes and report
            training and test error rates for both models.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task xml:id="hw06-q3-b" workspace="0.8in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Compare the training error rates of the restricted
            and unrestricted trees. Which is higher?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task xml:id="hw06-q3-c" workspace="0.8in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Compare their test error rates. Which model
            performs better on the held-out data?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task xml:id="hw06-q3-d" workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Explain why the two trees may have different
            training and test performance.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>
  </page>

  <page>
    <exercise xml:id="hw06-q4">
      <title>Question 4: Random Forest</title>
      <introduction>
        <p>Use the same training and test sets as in Question 1.</p>
      </introduction>
      <task xml:id="hw06-q4-a">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Fit a random forest predicting <c>Purchase</c>
            from the other variables, using at least 500 trees.
            Report its test error rate and display the variable
            importance values.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task xml:id="hw06-q4-b" workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Compare the random forest with the trees in
            Question 3 using test error rates. Identify the most
            important predictors and briefly interpret their
            importance values.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>
  </page>

  <page>
    <exercise xml:id="hw06-q5">
      <title>Question 5: Reflection</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write a short paragraph addressing all three questions:
          What is one advantage of a single decision tree?
          What is one disadvantage? Why can an ensemble method
          such as a random forest often improve prediction?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>
